{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3062","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-content-identity-control-set.md@sha256:e206451a5b9e3de17f1548c5e0d877cec88faf91ce46b0cfbb30cf3232bd79b3"},"owner_reference":"3062","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nContent-identity-addressed control set for the markdown consumption engine (implements R8 of the agent-markdown-consumption contract)\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nPagination responses today carry only a page number and total \u2014 no content identity. Across stateless invocations, a caller cannot request \"the next page of what you just returned\"; every page request re-collects from the provider and re-parses. This is also a correctness gap: if source content changes between page requests, pages from two different versions can be silently served as though they were one document.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. A response backed by the markdown engine's Navigation stage carries a stable content identifier (`content_id`), the command that produced it, and a generation timestamp, visible to the caller.\n2. A later page/navigate request is made using that `content_id` plus a page selector or ordinal address.\n3. Serving a later page from the control set never re-collects from the source and never redoes Generation \u2014 it cheaply reparses the already-stored raw content in memory.\n4. A request whose stored entry is marked stale is reported as such and auto-regenerates via the stored command, rather than being silently served.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. NOT a durable reuse-optimization cache across unrelated tasks or sessions \u2014 it is temporary per-task navigation scratch space. Reuse across follow-up pages within one task is the design intent, not general caching.\n2. NOT session-scoped. No `session_id` anywhere in the design \u2014 this was proposed (ADR-3075-4) and explicitly reversed (ADR-3082-1). Also not per-tool, per-subcommand, or per-transport \u2014 one global store for every caller.\n3. NOT skipping writes on identity match \u2014 every full backend request always regenerates and always writes a fresh row.\n4. NOT an in-process cache (module-level dict, MCP server lifespan context) \u2014 the CLI is a fresh OS process per invocation with no memory of prior calls, so storage must be out-of-process regardless of transport.\n5. NOT unbounded storage \u2014 bounded by a soft global size target with LRU eviction, not an entry-count cap.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. Storage: one SQLite database at `state_root()/control-set.db` (WAL mode), rows keyed by `content_id` alone.\n2. `content_id` is derived from the command (source, scope, parameters) plus the Generation stage's output for that command \u2014 not a hash of raw source alone, and not a hash of generated content alone (two different commands can coincidentally produce byte-identical generated output).\n3. Any write reachable from any caller that could invalidate a stored entry marks it `stale=true` (a marker column, never a row delete) \u2014 the stored command is preserved so a subsequent stale hit can auto-regenerate without the caller re-supplying the original scope.\n4. A caller may explicitly request revalidation or forced refresh via a `refresh` selector (`revalidate` | `force`). Both always write a fresh row; they differ only in what is reported \u2014 revalidation compares freshly computed identity to the previous one and reports whether it changed, forced refresh skips that comparison.\n5. Eviction: a periodic, rate-limited (hourly-to-daily, not per-write) sweep enforces a 40MB soft global size target via LRU-by-size trimming. `last_accessed_at` is bumped on read so an entry an agent is actively paging through is unlikely (best-effort, not guaranteed) to be evicted mid-navigation.\n6. A single entry larger than the 40MB target is let through as an explicit, documented exception rather than rejected.\n7. Concurrent-writer safety: the write sequence should be wrapped in an explicit SQLite transaction \u2014 full solution deliberately deferred to #3081 (tracked separately, not blocking this item's core scope).\n8. The invalidation mechanism must expose a callable/importable entrypoint other write paths can hook into \u2014 not just invalidation triggered from within this engine's own write calls. Confirmed external consumer: `task_status_hook.py` (a SAM task-status write path, touched by the in-flight #2787 Codex-compatibility PR) will need to call into this same invalidation entrypoint once #3062 lands, per a cross-session architectural-alignment check run against ADR-3075-2. Design the invalidation trigger as a reusable function/method other write paths can call, not internal-only logic embedded in this engine's own request handlers.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. Out-of-process persistence is required \u2014 the CLI has no long-running process memory to hold an in-process cache in.\n2. Global, not per-transport \u2014 MCP, CLI, and any other frontend caller resolve to the same entry for the same `content_id`.\n3. Disposable \u2014 the control set holds no authoritative data; every row is rebuildable from Collection + Generation on demand, so losing the whole database costs nothing but a cold cache.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\nNone outstanding. The two items the backlog entry itself listed under \"Open Decisions\" are both resolved (see Resolved Questions below), not open.\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` (R1-R8, especially R8)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-1-content-identity-and-cache-scope.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-2-stale-cache-recovery-and-write-invalidation.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-3-cache-metadata-visible-to-agent.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-4-out-of-process-session-keyed-control-set.md` (superseded in part by ADR-3082-1)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3082-1-sqlite-backed-bounded-eviction.md` (current authoritative design \u2014 read this one first)\n- `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md` (Control set, Backend, Frontend glossary entries)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` (component ownership map)\n- `task_status_hook.py` (external write-path consumer of this engine's invalidation entrypoint \u2014 see Functional Requirement 8; exact repo path to be confirmed during planning, flagged via cross-session alignment check against PR #2787)\n- #3081 (concurrent-writer transaction wrapping, deliberately deferred, tracked separately)\n- #3078 (artifact_read must also route through this engine once built)\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Cache scope: session-lived or persisted? | Persisted, content-keyed, global \u2014 NOT session-scoped | ADR-3082-1 (supersedes ADR-3075-4) |\n| Does the identifier derive from raw markdown or the parsed tree? | Neither alone \u2014 derived from the command (source+scope+params) plus Generation's output | ADR-3075-1 |\n| What storage mechanism? | One global SQLite DB at `state_root()/control-set.db`, WAL mode | ADR-3082-1 |\n| What eviction policy? | Soft 40MB size target, LRU by size, periodic rate-limited (hourly-to-daily) cleanup | ADR-3082-1 |\n| Does every request always regenerate and write? | Yes \u2014 no skip-write-on-identity-match optimization anywhere | ADR-3075-3 |\n| How is staleness detected and handled? | Write-triggered marking only (`stale=true`, row preserved); a separate read-time hash-mismatch check was considered and rejected as redundant | ADR-3075-2 (as corrected this session) |\n| Is the stored content parsed or raw? | Raw content only \u2014 every read (including revalidation) reparses in memory; no parsed-tree cache | ADR-3082-1, ADR-3075-3 |\n| Does anything outside this engine need to trigger invalidation? | Yes \u2014 `task_status_hook.py` will need to, once #3062 lands (confirmed by independent cross-session review) | Cross-session alignment check re: PR #2787, ADR-3075-2 |\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone blocking. Planning should account for #3081 (concurrent-writer locking) as an adjacent, dependent piece of work \u2014 ADR-3082-1 explicitly leaves it deferred, not decided within this item's scope. Planning should also locate `task_status_hook.py`'s exact current path/shape as part of impact-radius analysis, since Functional Requirement 8 depends on it.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Discovery content is drawn entirely from ADRs already reviewed, independently verified, and merged this session (PR #3080) \u2014 no new interactive discovery was needed. Presenting to user for a final sanity check before proceeding to planning.\n"}